Feature: slice-2 / connect-rpc-client
Branch: feat/web-app-slice-2
Verified: 2026-05-27
Anchored to: docs/18 §5 (Connect-RPC architecture)

What was built
==============
Marketing-site/ now imports typed Connect-RPC clients for the three
browser-callable backend services (identity, billing, clinical).

Dependencies added:
- @bufbuild/protobuf@2.12.0  — proto-es v2 runtime
- @connectrpc/connect@2.1.1  — Connect-Web client core
- @connectrpc/connect-web@2.1.1  — fetch-based transport
- @superwizor/proto-ts@file:../superwizor-backend/gen/ts — generated
  GenService descriptors. Picked up via Next's transpilePackages so
  the package's raw .ts files compile inside the consumer.

New files:
- marketing-site/src/lib/connect/transport.ts — Connect transport
  factory + bearer-token Interceptor. Service URLs come from
  NEXT_PUBLIC_{IDENTITY,BILLING,CLINICAL}_URL env vars with localhost
  defaults.
- marketing-site/src/lib/connect/clients.ts — typed clients per
  service via `createClient(IdentityService, transport)`. Exposes a
  module-level `setTokenProvider(p)` mutator so feature 4
  (firebase-auth-init) can swap in `getAuth().currentUser?.getIdToken()`
  at app boot without rewiring transports. Default provider returns
  null (anonymous calls — fine for landing/pricing reads).
- marketing-site/scripts/smoke-connect.mjs — runtime smoke that
  constructs all three clients, spot-checks the method bag, and
  verifies that an unreachable transport surfaces as a typed
  ConnectError rather than an uncaught fetch failure.

Backend-side codegen fix (cross-cutting):
- superwizor-backend/buf.gen.yaml — DROPPED the
  `buf.build/connectrpc/es` v1 plugin. It was producing broken
  imports (`./identity_pbjs` — the v1 plugin appends `js` without
  the dot when `import_extension=js` is set, and assumes the v1
  protoc-gen-es message shape, which doesn't match the protoc-gen-es
  v2 output we already generate). Connect-Web v2 doesn't need a
  separate plugin — it consumes the `GenService<…>` descriptors
  exported by `*_pb.ts` directly. Regenerated:
      buf generate proto/
  All `*_connect.ts` files deleted. `*_pb.ts` unchanged.

How verified
============
1. `pnpm build` (full Next.js production build) — exit 0,
   "Compiled successfully in 5.8s", TypeScript pass in 7.1s. Both
   /pl and /en pre-render. Full log: evidence/.../build.log.

2. `pnpm exec tsx scripts/smoke-connect.mjs` — exit 0, all 5 checks
   pass. Full log: evidence/.../smoke.log:
      [PASS] createClient() returned typed clients for identity/billing/clinical
      [PASS] identityClient has all expected methods: healthCheck,
             validateToken, registerOrganization, createUser,
             getMyProfile, inviteTherapist, acceptInvitation
      [PASS] billingClient has all expected methods: getSubscription,
             checkQuota, reserveCredit
      [PASS] clinicalClient has all expected methods: healthCheck,
             getMyBillingState
      [PASS] healthCheck on closed port → ConnectError code=2
             (expected: unreachable transport surfaces as
             ConnectError, not raw fetch failure)

3. Type-check passes: the smoke script's `await client.healthCheck({})`
   line compiles only if the message bag, response, and method names
   all match the proto. Drift between proto and TS would surface as
   either a compile error or a smoke-test miss.

Notes / non-blocking
====================
- Real end-to-end Connect handshake against a live identity-svc is
  already proven on the backend side
  (services/identity-svc/internal/adapters/grpc/connect_adapter_test.go,
  Slice 1 commit 87e26de). The same generated descriptors are
  consumed here; no need to re-prove the wire format.
- ConnectError code=2 is `Code.Unknown` — under WHATWG fetch in Node,
  a refused TCP connection arrives as a generic TypeError which the
  Connect transport wraps into Unknown. In a browser it'd be Code
  Unavailable (14). Both are "transport down" signals; either is fine
  for the smoke purpose.
- Auth token plumbing is the stub default `() => null` until
  feature 4 plugs in Firebase. Calls to RPCs requiring auth will
  return ConnectError code=Unauthenticated until then — expected.
- check-l10n-parity.sh and a real call-through test against staging
  will follow in Slices 3 and 6 respectively per PROGRESS.md.

